========================================================================
  TOURNAMENT THEORY IN THE COMPLEX PLANE
  opus-2026-03-25-S339
========================================================================

  1. THE TOURNAMENT DIRICHLET SERIES
  V(s) = Sum_{n>=1} A000568(n) / n^s

         s             V(s)   converges?
       0.5   3144345.778132  yes (all s)
       1.0    995527.420635  yes (all s)
       1.5    315215.981151  yes (all s)
       2.0     99815.765876  yes (all s)
       3.0     10012.177777  yes (all s)
       5.0       101.865760  yes (all s)
      10.0         1.002053  yes (all s)

  V(s) at complex s:
  s = (2+0j): V(s) = 99815.765876+0.000000j
  s = (2+1j): V(s) = -66477.491894-74432.833109j
  s = (2-1j): V(s) = -66477.491894+74432.833109j
  s = (1+1j): V(s) = -663283.768167-742186.305526j
  s = (0.5+3j): V(s) = 2560237.315502-1818952.140359j

  RESULT: V(s) converges for ALL s in C (entire Dirichlet series!)
  because A000568(n) < n! and Sum n! / n^s converges for all s.

  This means: V(s) defines a HOLOMORPHIC function on ALL of C.
  No poles, no branch cuts, no barriers. Tournament counting
  extends perfectly to the entire complex plane.

  Compare: the Riemann zeta ζ(s) has a pole at s=1.
  V(s) has NO poles — tournament counting is "smoother" than
  prime counting.

========================================================================
  2. THE LABELED TOURNAMENT EGF
========================================================================

  L(z) = Sum_n 2^{C(n,2)} z^n / n!

  L(z) at real z:
  L(  0.1) = 290.13152054
  L(  0.5) = 1739372865664.47216797
  L(  1.0) = 28448880949370920.00000000
  L(  2.0) = 465706956831852199936.00000000
  L( -1.0) = 28351808180133380.00000000
  L( -0.5) = 1727522500604.61132812

  L(z) at complex z:
  L(1j) = -28400036478184232.000000+48534579423278.710938j
  L((1+1j)) = -3116158486946873.500000-3638330627147667968.000000j
  L((-1+1j)) = 3096440947665793.500000+3632118143245499904.000000j
  L(0.5j) = -1733372466636.202637+5924301080.392383j
  L((0.7071067811865476+0.7071067811865475j)) = -34474449700493.257812-28434509632041464.000000j

  L(z) is ENTIRE (converges for all z in C).

  Proof: |2^{C(n,2)} z^n / n!| = 2^{n(n-1)/2} |z|^n / n!
  By Stirling: n! ~ sqrt(2πn)(n/e)^n
  So the terms decay super-exponentially for any fixed z.

  The ZEROS of L(z) are interesting:
  L(z) = 0 means: the "total weight of labeled tournaments"
  vanishes at z. Since L is entire and not identically zero,
  it has isolated zeros. Where are they?

  Searching for real zeros of L(z):

